        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Put the treadmill where you will use it, on a ground floor or
            dry basement if you can, with the clearance zone clear, the ceiling checked at full
            incline, a grounded outlet within reach and a mat underneath.</strong> Upstairs,
            bedrooms, garages and apartments all work; each costs you something specific, and the
            sections above say what.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Measure before you move anything.</strong> Tape out the
            footprint and the fall zone, run the ceiling arithmetic with your own model's figures,
            and check the route in. If the room is fixed and the machine is not, start from our
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">dimensions and space requirements guide</a>
            and let the space choose the treadmill.
          </p>
